@style(IDocDemo)
@title(Citations, glossary and index)

Cite sources, define terms, and build an index.

@section(Citations)

@subsection(@@cite)

Cite a reference → [n], numbered in first-citation order — with a page (p=12, pp=3-7, ch=, sec=), or as the author's name (text: "Shannon [1]"), the year or the author alone.

@cite(shannon48)

@cite(a, b)

@cite(shannon48, p=12)

@cite(shannon48, text)

@cite(shannon48, year)

@subsection(@@nocite)

Include a reference in the bibliography without citing it in the text.

@nocite(shannon48)

@subsection(@@references)

Define references inline — one per line, key: full text.

@references
shannon48: C. E. Shannon, …
@end

See the @@bibliography section above for @@references(group=, title=).

@subsection(@@bibliography)

Print the reference list — from a .bib file or from references.

@bibliography(file=17-citations-glossary-index.assets/sources.bib)

@bibliography

@references(group=primary, title=Primary sources)
[ref1] A. Rivera. Notes on small circuits. Example Press, 2024.
@end

@bibliography(group=primary, title=Primary sources)

A .bib file in a named style: @bibliography(17-citations-glossary-index.assets/sources.bib, style=ieee)

@section(Glossary and index)

@subsection(@@gloss)

A glossary term in the text, linked to its definition (and listed by printglossary).

@subsection(@@glossary)

The glossary's definitions, one term: definition per line (a term may have spaces).

@glossary
API: application programming interface
Fourier transform: …
@end
The @idx(entropy) of a source
a @idx(code, entry=coding)

@subsection(@@printglossary)

The glossary: the terms used, alphabetically, with their definitions.

@printglossary

@subsection(@@idx)

A word in the text that also goes into the index; entry= files it under another heading.

@subsection(@@printindex)

The index: every idx entry, alphabetical under its initial, with the pages it is on — each a link.

@printindex
